  "summary": "At a Nov. 18 Olympia City Council meeting, about a dozen residents urged the city to end its Flock Safety ALPR contract, citing privacy, immigration-enforcement, and public-records concerns following a Skagit County court ruling that Flock images are public records. Interim Chief Shelby Parker is scheduled to brief the council on Dec. 2; no decision meeting is yet scheduled, and some council members signaled openness to ending the contract.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "Keeping Flock means a tidal wave of public record requests, and with each request, the poor clerks are further behind on responding to other requests.",
      "paragraph_idx": 17
    },
    {
      "quote": "if a single public records request can yield more than 2 million images, then anyone can access sensitive information",
      "paragraph_idx": 25
    },
    {
      "quote": "he's in support of the council ending its contract with the system",
      "paragraph_idx": 26
    }
  ],
